## Generic Types

Generic functions can also be defined for multiple types as long as these types satisfy some constraints. As in Go, a set of constraints is defined by defining a new type as an interface and listing the types satisfying the constraint. In the following example, the type constraints `ints` is defined as a `int32` or a `int64`.

```
type ints interface {
    int32 | int64
}
```

In the example, we define two constraints `ints` and `floats`, and a function that converts a value with a type satisfying `ints` into a type satisfying `floats`.

Whenever possible, the compiler will use arguments to infer the type parameters of the function, as in the second call to `toFloats` in the example.

```code:main
package main

type ints interface {
    int32 | int64
}

type floats interface {
    float32 | float64
}

func toFloats[Dest floats, Src ints](a Src) Dest {
    return Dest(a)
}

func Main() (float32, float64) {
    a := int64(2) 
    f32a := toFloats[float32, int64](a)
    // In the following call, the compiler uses type inference
    // to determine the type of the function parameter Src from its argument.
    f64a := toFloats[float64](a)
    return f32a, f64a
}
```

